WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:08.600
Hi everyone, I'm Tanja, welcome to my channel. I do drawing and painting, and on this channel we do exactly that.

2
00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:18.800
If you're interested in these topics, watch some of my past videos and subscribe to the channel to find out when I post a new one.

3
00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:28.400
Today we're drawing in my art journal, or illustrated diary, which I talked about in one of my past videos.

4
00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:36.500
It's like a diary, only with illustrations and a small amount of text.

5
00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:47.500
I started keeping this diary last year, when my baby was very small, and I had no time for more complex illustrations.

6
00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:57.700
I really wanted to draw, and so I started keeping this diary and drawing different things from my everyday life.

7
00:00:57.700 --> 00:01:06.100
Sometimes they're interesting things, sometimes boring ones, but when you draw them, even things that seem boring to us

8
00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:16.700
become interesting, and we stop taking them for granted, and they become beautiful, entertaining and unusual.

9
00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:25.600
I haven't decided yet what I'll draw today, but it will be something from the fair in Ruma,

10
00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:31.700
which we went to this weekend, so now I'll look through

11
00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:39.200
what I photographed and filmed there, and choose what made the strongest impression on me,

12
00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:45.000
and then I'll draw it, and you'll be with me and see how I do it.

13
00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:27.500
So, I've come up with what we're going to draw. There, you saw it, here it is, this is my mini sketch: what we'll draw and how this spread in the art journal will look.

14
00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:36.600
As I've said before, some trifles become important when you draw them,

15
00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:43.000
so this time I'll draw the ladle I bought at the fair.

16
00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:52.900
I'd been looking for a ladle for a long time that isn't like this big one, and finally found

17
00:03:52.900 --> 00:04:07.200
a narrow one at the fair: when I cook something in a small saucepan, it's narrow and deep, and I need a narrower ladle so as not to scratch it with a metal spoon.

18
00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:10.900
So here, I bought a ladle and I'll draw it.

19
00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:22.400
The second thing is an orange lemon, which I'd never seen before. I don't know if it's a well-known thing,

20
00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:29.800
but it seems to be some variety of lemon of ours, orange, that grows here. I first saw it at the fair.

21
00:04:29.800 --> 00:04:39.300
The seller says: lemon. I thought it was some kind of mandarin or orange, but it's a lemon, so I'll draw that

22
00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:50.800
and I'll draw the T-shirt that Vuk, my husband's nephew, got from his mom at the fair.

23
00:04:50.800 --> 00:05:03.600
They printed a pike on his T-shirt right on the spot, because he adores fishing and chose a pike to be printed on the T-shirt, so I'll draw that

24
00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:10.700
and pancakes, because Vuk also got a pancake

25
00:05:10.700 --> 00:05:18.800
when we went home. I watched and even filmed a man making pancakes, so I'll draw that too.

26
00:05:19.300 --> 00:05:26.100
If, of course, in the meantime something doesn't change and I don't decide to draw something else instead of what I said.

27
00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:30.500
So for now that's the plan, well, let's draw.

28
00:08:54.900 --> 00:09:09.200
And here I am: I've finished the main pencil sketch, now it's the turn of the lines — I do them with fineliners, which are thin markers. I like to do the lines in brown, because black is too strong for the delicate watercolor

29
00:09:09.200 --> 00:09:16.700
that I'll color everything with afterwards. Here I have Micron markers, fineliners

30
00:09:16.700 --> 00:09:25.900
and Unipin fineliners. Both are sepia colors, only these are still a bit paler than those.

31
00:09:26.900 --> 00:09:36.900
I haven't decided yet which ones I'll draw with, I think I'll take Unipin, because I haven't tried them yet, they're new.

32
00:09:36.900 --> 00:09:50.900
Here I have thickness 0.1 and a "brush," which is supposed to be a bit like a brush, but no, it's more like a slightly more flexible marker.

33
00:09:50.900 --> 00:10:04.900
Unlike these Micron ones, where the brush pen is still more like a brush than the Unipin.

34
00:10:04.900 --> 00:10:13.900
So now we'll try the Unipin, and if somewhere darker lines are needed, we'll combine it with the Micron.

35
00:10:13.900 --> 00:10:15.900
Well, let's see.

36
00:12:50.900 --> 00:12:56.000
Here we are together again, I've finished the lines. In the end I used only this one Unipin fineliner with 0.1 thickness, I didn't need anything else.

37
00:12:56.000 --> 00:13:04.000
I did both the text and the drawings with it. At the end I only erased the graphite pencil lines, and now all that's left is to color.

38
00:13:04.000 --> 00:13:15.900
For coloring I'll use my watercolors in this palette, I usually carry it everywhere. It says "cosmetics" on it,

39
00:13:15.900 --> 00:13:28.800
but inside it's not cosmetics, I put Van Gogh watercolors in there. I'll clean the palette a bit, because last time I didn't clean it after working, and then we'll color.

40
00:16:53.200 --> 00:17:00.600
And here we've reached the end. I've finished one page of my diary and, most importantly, captured one memory

41
00:17:00.600 --> 00:17:12.800
from life. I hope you enjoyed watching this, and I hope I've inspired someone to start an illustrated diary.

42
00:17:13.800 --> 00:17:19.300
Well, that's all from me for today. I'm going for a walk with my little one.

43
00:17:19.500 --> 00:17:24.600
The day is beautiful, spring is coming to us, and we'll see each other in the next video. Bye!